From: Roman Senn <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] client: h1: restart the requests queued on a connection that turned out not to be kept alive

With LCCSCF_PIPELINE, a second (third...) client request to an endpoint that
already has a connection is queued behind the first one on that connection
instead of opening its own.  If the first response then shows that the
server does not keep the connection alive (an HTTP/1.0 response, or an
HTTP/1.1 one with neither a content length nor chunking, whose body ends
with the connection), the queued requests cannot go out on it.  The code
that notices this in lws_client_interpret_server_handshake() sends each
queued wsi back to LRS_UNCONNECTED, "to go back to trying to connect", and
that is where it stops: nothing starts that connect.  The queued requests
are never sent, never fail, and no callback is made for them: the
application never hears from them again, unless it has a timeout of its
own.

An application that makes several requests at once to such a server
(python's http.server answers HTTP/1.0 by default, for example) and uses
LCCSCF_PIPELINE to share a connection loses every request but the first.

Cause
-----

For a redirect, the same restart is done correctly:
lws_client_reset() leaves the wsi unconnected and the close flow
(_lws_close_free_wsi(..., "redir")) re-attaches a header table with
lws_header_table_attach(), which sees LRS_UNCONNECTED and goes on with
the connect (lws_client_connect_2_dnsreq_MAY_CLOSE_WSI()).  The pipeline
restart sends the state back with
lws_wsi_event_role(ww, LWS_WSIEV_RESTART, rops) but does not do that last
step, so nothing follows.

The fix
-------

Collect the restarted wsi on a local list while the loop holds the vhost
lock and, once the lock is dropped, call lws_header_table_attach() for
each of them: for a client in LRS_UNCONNECTED it goes on to make the
connection, as in the redirect case.  That can close and free the wsi
(and it takes the vhost lock itself), which is why it is not done inside
the loop over the queue.  A failure to attach is logged.

Reproducer
----------

A standalone C program: a child process serving plain HTTP/1.0 responses
(one connection each, the body ends where the connection does, the server
hangs up 200 ms after answering), and an lws client context that makes two
GETs with LCCSCF_PIPELINE from the same loop turn.  Attached as
h1-pipeline-no-keepalive.c; build with

    cc -o repro h1-pipeline-no-keepalive.c -I<lws>/include -I<build> \
       <build>/lib/libwebsockets.a -lz -lcap -lpthread

    $ ./repro                          # before
    1 of 2 requests completed, 0 failed                (exit 1, waits 5s)
    $ ./repro                          # after
    2 of 2 requests completed, 0 failed                (exit 0)

The first response completes in both cases; the second request is
connected and sent only with the patch (strace shows the second connect()
and the second request on it).  "0 failed" before is the point: no
LWS_CALLBACK_CLIENT_CONNECTION_ERROR is made either.

Tested on x86_64 Linux, poll event loop, no TLS, against current main.
Made against libwebsockets f1650e3e7 (main).

Signed-off-by: Roman Senn <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>

---
diff --git a/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c b/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c
index 7693efef5..ae232fc2b 100644
--- a/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c
+++ b/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c
@@ -1482,6 +1482,7 @@ lws_client_interpret_server_handshake(struct lws *wsi)
 			 * to be computed outside the event macro's argument
 			 * list, MSVC rejects #if inside one (C5101)
 			 */
+			lws_dll2_owner_t restarted;
 			const struct lws_role_ops *rops =
 #if defined(LWS_ROLE_H1)
 						   &role_ops_h1;
@@ -1509,6 +1510,7 @@ lws_client_interpret_server_handshake(struct lws *wsi)
 			 */
 			wsi->keepalive_rejected = 1;
 
+			lws_dll2_owner_clear(&restarted);
 			lws_vhost_lock(wsi->a.vhost);
 			lws_start_foreach_dll_safe(struct lws_dll2 *,
 						   d, d1,
@@ -1525,8 +1527,30 @@ lws_client_interpret_server_handshake(struct lws *wsi)
 				/* go back to "trying to connect" state */
 				lws_wsi_event_role(ww, LWS_WSIEV_RESTART, rops);
 				ww->user_space = NULL;
+
+				/* park him until we are out of the vhost lock */
+				lws_dll2_add_tail(&ww->dll2_cli_txn_queue,
+						  &restarted);
 			} lws_end_foreach_dll_safe(d, d1);
 			lws_vhost_unlock(wsi->a.vhost);
+
+			/*
+			 * Nothing else will start these: attaching a header
+			 * table to a client in LRS_UNCONNECTED goes on to do
+			 * the connect, same as for a redirect's restart.  That
+			 * can close and free him, and it takes the vhost lock
+			 * itself, so it is done here and not in the loop
+			 */
+			lws_start_foreach_dll_safe(struct lws_dll2 *, d, d1,
+					lws_dll2_get_head(&restarted)) {
+				struct lws *ww = lws_container_of(d, struct lws,
+						dll2_cli_txn_queue);
+
+				lws_dll2_remove(&ww->dll2_cli_txn_queue);
+				if (lws_header_table_attach(ww, 0) ==
+						LWS_AH_ATTACH_FAIL)
+					lwsl_wsi_err(ww, "failed to restart queued");
+			} lws_end_foreach_dll_safe(d, d1);
 		}
 	}
 
